  "apple_responses": {
    "apple-cloud": {
      "model_requested": "cloud",
      "model_used": "cloud",
      "response": "The plan still omits explicit handling of email deliverability beyond a single retry. Because confirmation is best-effort and residents receive no alternative channel, a persistent bounce or spam filter could leave them without proof of booking. This matters: without a way to re-send or verify receipt, volunteers might face disputes over attendance or no-shows, undermining trust in a free service. I would add a dashboard indicator showing whether confirmation succeeded and allow volunteers to resend via the same magic link; this stays within scope by reusing existing access and avoids resident accounts or SMS."
    },
    "apple-cloud-pro": {
      "model_requested": "cloud-pro",
      "model_used": "cloud-pro",
      "response": "Cloud's recommendation introduces a false sense of security. A simple send API only confirms that the email left the server, not that it bypassed the resident's spam filter or reached their inbox. Displaying a \"confirmation succeeded\" indicator on the dashboard confuses send success with actual delivery.\n\nInstead of tracking unreliable delivery states, we can close the gap by displaying the booking details and a unique confirmation code directly on the post-submission success page. This provides immediate, verifiable proof of booking without relying on email deliverability or adding new dashboard indicators.\n\n**Proposed Change:**\nUpdate the success page to display the full booking details and a unique confirmation code (e.g., the first 8 characters of the booking ID). Advise residents to screenshot or print this page as their proof of booking.\n\n**Concrete Test:**\n- **Test:** Submit a valid booking and verify that the success page displays the correct slot time, volunteer name, and a matching unique confirmation code, even if the background email service is completely disabled."
    }
  },
